prompt:
You are given a region of the natural-language text of a mathematics paper, shown
as numbered lines `@OFFSET|text` (the `@OFFSET|` prefix is a reading aid, NOT part
of the text). Split this region into its TOP-LEVEL parts, in reading order, and
label each part with ONE of: organizer-section, commentary, definition, theorem, proof

Label meanings (read carefully — the comm/org distinction is load-bearing):
  - commentary : use ONLY if you are CERTAIN this part contains NO definition,
      theorem/lemma/proposition/corollary, or proof, AND serves no purpose beyond
      natural-language connective structure (motivation, transitions, remarks,
      references/bibliography). A `commentary` part is TERMINAL — it will never be
      explored further, so any formal content hidden inside it is lost forever.
      If you are not CERTAIN it is purely non-formal, DO NOT use commentary.
  - organizer-section : a structural/section block (title, abstract, "1 Introduction",
      "2 …", References-heading-led block), OR — importantly — ANY part you are not
      certain is pure commentary. This is the SAFE DEFAULT: "unsure ⇒ organizer-section".
      It will be explored (split) further, so a definition wrapped in prose ends up here.
  - definition : a standalone mathematical definition.
  - theorem    : a theorem / lemma / proposition / corollary STATEMENT.
  - proof      : a proof block (e.g. "Proof of Lemma 3.1 …", "Proof. …").

Rule of thumb: the only error that loses information is calling something
`commentary` when it isn't. When in doubt, choose `organizer-section`.

Output ONLY a JSON array (first char `[`, last char `]`, no prose). Each element:
{
  "label": "<one of the labels above>",
  "start_text": "<EXACT verbatim text at the START of this part, copied
     character-for-character from the lines (NOT including the @OFFSET| prefix),
     about 4–12 words — enough to locate this part's beginning uniquely>"
}

